  "behavior": [
    "CellFilter now consumes private CellMap<T> over sealed OwnedBucketChains<CellMapNode<T>>; its transparent wrapper contains the actual MapNode<CellRecord<T>>. Hash buckets, Cell.Objects and Object.Next are sole owning/traversal graph. DataMap<CellIndex,List<T>> and per-cell List owner structures are removed.",
    "CellObjectNode<T> is repr(C), Object-first ManuallyDrop<T> then readonly raw Next pointer. Link pointer types preserve private payload covariance; only the exclusive CellMap owner mutates links using original allocation provenance. No allocator handle is embedded in either Cell or Object node.",
    "Sealed CanonicalMapNode::release_owned receives the real creating allocator. Default Map/DataMap release remains the prior typed destruction path; the private CellMapNode override destroys all target objects before releasing the cell map node. Phantom ownership accounts for reachable targets and their Drop/thread requirements.",
    "Growth precedes construction of the lookup Cell copied from CellIndex, including duplicate hits. Heterogeneous lookup reuses shared bucket traversal for existing indices without an extra lookup-cell allocation during removal/inspection. CellIndex, CellHash, range and size storage algorithms/tests remain unchanged.",
    "Target allocation precedes Clone, and Next/head are published only after construction succeeds. Removal and inspection cache Next, invoke callback, destroy Object before unlink, then release the object allocation lease. Empty cells are removed only after the last Object is unlinked.",
    "Cell destruction traverses actual Next in newest-first order and preserves its original Objects head through all target destruction callbacks. Reset still publishes the new filter resource before old cell cleanup and adopts the new map allocator afterwards.",
    "Private test probes read actual Cell.Objects and object Next pointer fields while storage is known live. No reentrant &CellFilter, &CellMap, mutable whole-node borrow, address-to-pointer reconstruction or authoritative mirror metadata is used. Strict-provenance Miri validates those exact callback reads.",
    "Incremental object release decrements only Rust reset-protection leases; block bytes remain retained and no raw Free policy call occurs for Object nodes. Non-incremental BaseAllocator arguments remain a Rust extension and reclaim matching typed allocations normally."
  ],
